{"y":2026,"label":"2025–26","coach":"Dan Hurley","conf":"Big East","confShort":"Big East","w":34,"l":6,"cw":17,"cl":3,"confFinish":"2nd in Big East","finish":"runner","seed":2,"region":"East","apPre":4,"apHigh":2,"apFinal":2,"srs":23.42,"sos":12.02,"ortg":115.8,"drtg":98.6,"pace":65.7,"ppg":76.6,"oppg":65.2,"story":{"headline":"The Mullins Miracle and an Eighth Final Four","text":"With transfers Silas Demary Jr. and Malachi Smith and freshman Braylon Mullins joining Alex Karaban, Tarris Reed Jr. and Solo Ball, UConn went 34–6. The Huskies beat No. 7 BYU in Boston, won at Kansas, beat Florida in the Jimmy V Classic and started 12–0 in the Big East, finishing second. Emeka Okafor's No. 50 was retired in February, and Karaban became the first active men's player inducted into the Huskies of Honor. Karaban, Demary and Reed made first-team All-Big East — the league's first trio from one team. After a 72–52 loss to St. John's in the Big East final, the No. 2 seed Huskies beat Furman (Reed: 31 points, 27 rebounds), UCLA and Michigan State, then erased a 19-point second-half deficit against No. 1 overall seed Duke: Demary deflected a pass, Karaban found Mullins, and Mullins buried a 35-foot three with 0.4 seconds left, 73–72. UConn beat Illinois in the Final Four before Michigan won the title game 69–63 — UConn's first loss in seven title games.","moments":[{"date":"2026-02-18","title":"Okafor's No. 50 retired","text":"Emeka Okafor's number is retired at halftime against Creighton at Gampel Pavilion."},{"date":"2026-02-28","title":"Karaban enters the Huskies of Honor","text":"Alex Karaban becomes the first active men's player inducted into the Huskies of Honor."},{"date":"2026-03-14","title":"Big East tournament Championship: L vs St. John's","text":"UConn lost to St. John's 52–72 (Championship) at Madison Square Garden, New York, NY."},{"date":"2026-03-20","title":"NCAA First round: W vs Furman","text":"UConn beat Furman 82–71 in the NCAA tournament first round at Xfinity Mobile Arena, Philadelphia, PA."},{"date":"2026-03-22","title":"NCAA Second round: W vs UCLA","text":"UConn beat UCLA 73–57 in the NCAA tournament second round at Xfinity Mobile Arena, Philadelphia, PA."},{"date":"2026-03-27","title":"NCAA Sweet Sixteen: W vs Michigan State","text":"UConn beat Michigan State 67–63 in the NCAA tournament sweet sixteen at Capital One Arena, Washington, D.C."},{"date":"2026-03-29","title":"The Mullins miracle","text":"Braylon Mullins' 35-foot three with 0.4 seconds left completes a 19-point comeback over No. 1 Duke, 73–72."},{"date":"2026-04-04","title":"NCAA Final Four: W vs Illinois","text":"UConn beat Illinois 71–62 in the NCAA tournament final four at Lucas Oil Stadium, Indianapolis, IN."},{"date":"2026-04-06","title":"NCAA National Championship: L vs Michigan","text":"UConn lost to Michigan 63–69 in the NCAA tournament national championship at Lucas Oil Stadium, Indianapolis, IN."}],"honors":["NCAA national runner-up","Tarris Reed Jr. – NCAA Regional Most Outstanding Player","Alex Karaban – First-team All-Big East","Silas Demary Jr. – First-team All-Big East","Tarris Reed Jr. – First-team All-Big East","NCAA Final Four"],"sources":["https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2025%E2%80%9326_UConn_Huskies_men's_basketball_team","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/UConn_Huskies_men%27s_basketball","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Braylon_Mullins","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Alex_Karaban","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Tarris_Reed_Jr.","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Silas_Demary_Jr."]},"roster":[{"pid":"alex-karaban-1","name":"Alex Karaban","num":"11","cls":"SR","pos":"F","ht":"6-8","wt":210,"home":"Southborough, MA","hs":"Algonquin Regional (MA)","photo":"https://a.espncdn.com/i/headshots/mens-college-basketball/players/full/4917149.png","pg":{"g":40,"gs":40,"mp":34.3,"pts":13.2,"trb":5.3,"ast":2.4,"stl":0.8,"blk":0.8,"tov":1.1,"fg_pct":0.464,"fg3_pct":0.374,"ft_pct":0.851,"orb":1.8,"pf":2.0},"tot":{"mp":1370,"fg":188,"fga":405,"fg3":77,"fg3a":206,"